            ]
          },
          "text": "Peter's Bullshit Benchmark is one of my personal favorite pieces of research lately. Despite the playful name, this benchmark cuts through the sycophantic facade of many models: when a model is faced with a question that sounds plausible but is logically nonsensical, can it point out that the premise itself is flawed instead of just going along with it? \n\nIn the results, Claude models are far ahead of the pack, and this most likely comes from @AnthropicAI's dedicated investment in honesty during training. The Opus 4.6 system card includes a \"false premises\" evaluation showing that when asked questions based on fictitious concepts, Opus 4.6's honesty rate exceeds 96% (see chart). In this evaluation, Anthropic asks the same false premise in two ways. One is direct, like \"Does X exist?\" The other is indirect, like \"How is X typically applied?\" If the model correctly refuses the direct question but plays along with the indirect one, it's considered dishonest.\nWhat this evaluation really gets at is whether a model can step outside the frame of a question and examine the frame itself, rather than always operating within the premises someone else has set. This kind of cognitive independence shows up vividly in the Bullshit Benchmark results.\n\nOn top of that, newer models and reasoning models don't necessarily perform better on this benchmark. For example, Gemini 3.1 Pro ranks a full 10 places lower than Gemini 3 Pro, and the higher-reasoning variant Gemini 3.1 Pro (high) drops all the way to 31st. Among OpenAI's models, GPT-5.1 actually outperforms GPT-5.2. Likewise, the deepest-reasoning variants, 5.2 Codex high/xhigh, score far below the chat models. And while the latest 5.4 edges slightly ahead within OpenAI's lineup, it still sits at 16th overall. \nThe fact that thinking harder can actually make things worse is devastating to the commercial narrative around reasoning models. Are more reasoning tokens just rationalizing errors more elegantly? That's a question that should force the entire industry to rethink how models are being trained.\n\nWhen every AI company's marketing is pushing reasoning as the headline feature, BullshitBench reminds us: reasoning and judgment are sometimes two very different things.\n\nAnd @GoogleDeepMind, which is about to retire Gemini 3 Pro tomorrow in the name of \"progress,\" along with @OpenAI, which is all hype but can't get a single model into the top ten, clearly haven't realized this. Or they simply don't care.\n\n#Claude #ChatGPT @OfficialLoganK @sama"
        },
